A U.S. REIT index fund invests in a diversified portfolio of publicly traded real estate investment trusts (REITs). These companies typically own or finance income-producing properties such as apartments, offices, retail centers, or industrial facilities. Investors can therefore participate in the U.S. real estate market without directly purchasing property.

REITs can provide income through distributions and may benefit from rising property values and rental income. A diversified REIT index fund also provides exposure to multiple real estate companies and property types.

However, REIT investments can fluctuate substantially in value. They are affected by changes in property markets, economic conditions, and interest rates. Rising interest rates can increase financing costs and make REITs less attractive relative to bonds. Property-sector downturns can also reduce both share prices and distributions.

The fund is generally liquid because its shares are traded on public markets. However, unlike owning a property directly, the value of the investment can change significantly over short periods.
